      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-density zoning typically allows for greater housing supply, which can help alleviate housing shortages and make housing more affordable. It also encourages sustainable development by promoting public transit use and walkability, reducing dependence on cars and lowering carbon footprints. However, it can sometimes lead to overcrowding, increased congestion, and a loss of neighborhood character and open space.\n\nSingle-family neighborhoods often provide more privacy and exclusive communities, with each home contributing to a sense of identity and stability. However, they can exacerbate housing shortages and affordability issues, limit mixed-use development, and potentially lead to segregation by socioeconomic status.\n\nModern planning trends increasingly favor high-density zoning as urban areas grow and the need for affordable housing and sustainable development becomes more urgent. As a proponent of high-density development, supporting modern planning trends and emphasizing the net positive impact on the community could strengthen your argument at the planning commission.",
